1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,392
Collecting ten thousand online contacts feels productive,

2
00:00:03,392 --> 00:00:05,416
yet primate brain biology caps how

3
00:00:05,416 --> 00:00:07,618
many real relationships any human can

4
00:00:07,618 --> 00:00:08,630
actually sustain.

5
00:00:08,630 --> 00:00:09,163
In 1992,

6
00:00:09,163 --> 00:00:12,092
Oxford anthropologist Robin Dunbar showed in

7
00:00:12,092 --> 00:00:14,423
the Journal of Human Evolution that

8
00:00:14,423 --> 00:00:17,286
neocortex size limits stable human networks

9
00:00:17,286 --> 00:00:19,683
to roughly one hundred fifty people,

10
00:00:19,683 --> 00:00:22,080
anchored by an inner circle of five.

11
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:24,757
Spreading attention thinly across noisy feeds

12
00:00:24,757 --> 00:00:27,077
starves both your five high-trust peers

13
00:00:27,077 --> 00:00:29,456
and what sociologist Mark Granovetter in

14
00:00:29,456 --> 00:00:31,300
1973 called valuable weak ties.

15
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:32,910
Build a two-tier network:

16
00:00:32,910 --> 00:00:35,485
invest eighty percent of your relational

17
00:00:35,485 --> 00:00:37,610
energy into five candid builders,

18
00:00:37,610 --> 00:00:39,928
and send two thoughtful no-ask notes

19
00:00:39,928 --> 00:00:41,860
weekly to respected weak ties.

20
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:44,524
Depth in your inner five builds resilience,

21
00:00:44,524 --> 00:00:47,001
while genuine curiosity across weak ties

22
00:00:47,001 --> 00:00:48,550
brings fresh opportunity.
